A||N||1||DaveL||r-134 and refrigerant oil||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||08-11-2000||04:04 PM||||Am in the process of installing a new a/c comp. in my 87 SE 2.5  The new compressor says it was shipped without oil. How much should it hold, and is there really a new refrigerant oil thats compatible with the old style? Also, wouldn't it be smart to install a new belt with it, and have the alternator checked to be sure its up to snuff?||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||08-11-2000||06:11 PM||||Ester (polyol ester) oil is somewhat compatible with mineral oil (which is what the old R12 systems use). That's also what's commonly in the pressurized cans of oil for R134.<P>Best way to do this is to put about 2 ounces of the ester oil into the compressor prior to installation and turn the compressor hub 12-15 times. Then reinstall the plug that came with the compressor and install compressor. Putting the plug in until installation keeps oil from coming back out as you're jockeying the compressor around and also minimizes the oil attracting moisture.<P>I just did this job on my '86...hope you're also changing the drier and orifice tube. And inspect your engine compartment hoses to be sure they're okay (found out the hard way I had a leak at the crimp).<P>The manuals aren't much help with this job...it eases the installation if you remove the heater hoses in the engine compartment and drop the cat converter heat shield (only held by three or four small bolts). Another help- is to loosen the dogbone and move the engine forward, then retighten the bone. This gives an extra inch or two to play with.<P>As to using a new belt...why would you not...unless your old one is less than a year old, don't mess with it.<P>If you're going to R134, might as well change out the O rings and blow out/flush the evaporator and condenser. Removing the condenser is easy...that's where a lot of your old oil will be. While it's out, you can also straighten the fins and clean the bugs/dirt out.<P>All these things together don't take but a few hours to do, and you'll have minimal problems down the road.<P>Oh, and system capacity is 8.5 ounces oil, and 24-28 ounces R134 should do the trick..<P>Stay cool, man...<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deceler8_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
